    "abstract": "Abstract North Atlantic sea surface temperatures have large climate impacts affecting the weather of the Northern Hemisphere. In addition to a substantial warming over much of the North Atlantic, caused by increasing greenhouse gases over the 21st century, climate projections show a surprising region of considerable future cooling at midlatitudes, referred to as the North Atlantic warming hole. A similar pattern of surface temperature trends has been observed in recent decades, but it remains unclear whether this pattern is of anthropogenic origin or a simple manifestation of internal climate variability. Here, analyzing state-of-the-art climate models and observations, we show that the recent North Atlantic warming hole is of anthropogenic origin. Our analysis reveals that the anthropogenic signal has only recently emerged from the internal climate variability, and can be attributed to greenhouse gas emissions. We further show that a declining northward oceanic heat flux in recent decades, which is linked to this surface temperature pattern, is also of anthropogenic origin.",
    "field": "Climate change attribution",
    "problem": "Determining the origin of the observed North Atlantic warming hole is challenging because the region's large internal climate variability can mask or mimic the signal from human-caused anthropogenic emissions.",
    "method": "The paper proposes a fingerprint detection analysis that calculates a signal-to-noise ratio by projecting observed sea surface temperature trends onto an anthropogenic fingerprint from climate model ensembles to separate the human-forced signal of the North Atlantic warming hole from internal climate variability.",
    "task": "The task is to determine if the recently observed North Atlantic warming hole is caused by human activity (anthropogenic) or is a manifestation of natural internal climate variability by analyzing climate models and observations to detect a human-caused signal.",
